INDEX = dict(
    title="Patagonia Hiking Plans",
    desc="Five day-hiking itineraries for Patagonia, Jan 27 – Feb 21 2027, by car from Balmaceda.",
    html='<header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Jan 27 – Feb 21 in Patagonia · 2 people · sleeping in the car · max €1,500 per person</p><h1>Patagonia day-hiking plans</h1>\n<p class="lede">Five ways for two people to spend 25 days hiking from Balmaceda, sleeping in the car. Every plan uses only day hikes on marked, non-technical trails (no alpine routes or exposed passes). Guided trips are left out, apart from two short optional extras I think are really worth it. Each plan keeps a few flex days for bad weather, because wind decides which days you can hike. Every plan page has an interactive map with the driving route and the trails, plus a GPX file to load into OsmAnd, Maps.me or Gaia GPS for offline use. Each plan page also has a <b>"Show GPT options"</b> switch that adds extra hikes from the Greater Patagonian Trail manual (in purple, without changing the plan). The scouting reports behind them are in <a href="gpt-reports/README.md">gpt-reports</a>.</p></header>\n<div class="plancards">{CARDS}</div>\n<h2>Side by side</h2>\n<div class="tscroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Hike days</th><th>Flex days</th><th>Driving days</th><th>Hiking km</th><th>Hiking ascent</th><th>Driving km</th><th>Overnight spots</th><th>Argentina</th><th>Budget</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ROWS}</tbody></table></div>\n<p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14px">"Hike days" counts days with at least one real hike (3 h or more). "Hiking km" and "Hiking ascent" add up all hikes in the plan (upper end of each range; boat trips and viewpoints not counted). Most driving and flex days also include short walks. "pp" = per person. The budgets cover everything on the ground (food, fuel, campgrounds, fees, ferries), but not flights or the rental car. Fuel and campgrounds are shared, so they are split between the two of you.</p>\n<h2>My recommendation for you</h2>\n<div class="grid2">\n<div class="card"><h3>Best fit: Plan 3, Aysén + El Chaltén</h3><p style="margin:0">You have the Argentina permit, you can hike back to back, and you sleep in the car. That makes <a href="plan-3-aysen-and-chalten.html">Plan 3</a> the best match: it has the most top-quality, safe, non-technical day hikes, and it costs only about €720 per person. El Chaltén trails start in town and need no guide. <a href="plan-4-grand-loop.html">Plan 4</a> adds Torres del Paine and Perito Moreno. It still fits your budget at about €900 per person, but it has fewer hiking days and many more hours behind the wheel.</p></div>\n<div class="card"><h3>Cheapest and most relaxed: Plan 2</h3><p style="margin:0">If you would rather keep money in reserve and drive less, <a href="plan-2-unhurried-south.html">Plan 2</a> stays in Chile with long stays at the best hiking bases. <a href="plan-1-end-of-the-road.html">Plan 1</a> is for the full road-trip experience, and <a href="plan-5-northern-rainforest.html">Plan 5</a> for volcanoes and rainforest.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h2>Staying safe without alpine terrain</h2>\n<div class="grid2">\n<div class="card"><h3>Weather is the real danger</h3><ul class="clean">\n<li>Wind gusts of 80–100 km/h on ridges and moraines are normal. Check <b>windy.com</b>, <b>meteoblue</b> and <b>mountain-forecast.com</b> every evening, and match the big hikes to the calm days (that is what the buffer days are for).</li>\n<li>Set a turnaround time and keep to it. Summer days are long (sunset around 21:30), but storms arrive quickly.</li>\n<li>Rivers rise in the afternoon when the snow melts. Never ford a river on foot that comes above your knees.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="card"><h3>Logistics that matter</h3><ul class="clean">\n<li><b>Car:</b> a high-clearance 4x4 or pickup, ideally with 2 spare tyres. Most of the Carretera south of Cerro Castillo is gravel.</li>\n<li><b>Sleeping in the car:</b> nights are 0–8 °C and windy. Bring sleeping bags rated around 0 °C, window covers for privacy and against condensation, and park with the nose into the wind. Paid campgrounds give you showers and a sheltered place to cook. Use them every 2–3 nights.</li>\n<li><b>Fuel:</b> fill up whenever you can. Stations are far apart (Villa O\'Higgins, Tortel and Ruta 40 especially). A jerrycan is worth having.</li>\n<li><b>No signal</b> on most trails and long stretches of road. Download offline maps (Maps.me / OsmAnd / Wikiloc) and consider a satellite messenger (inReach or similar).</li>\n<li><b>Register</b> at CONAF ranger stations where asked (Cerro Castillo has a latest start time).</li>\n<li><b>Cash:</b> many small places take cash only, and ATMs run out at weekends.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="card"><h3>Book now (January and February are peak season)</h3><ul class="clean">\n<li>The rental car (4x4, plus the Argentina permit if needed)</li>\n<li>Santiago ⇄ Balmaceda flights (for the return, fly on Feb 21, not on the 22nd)</li>\n<li>The Chile Chico ⇄ Puerto Ibáñez car ferry (Somarco)</li>\n<li>Park tickets online: pasesparques.cl (Cerro Castillo, Queulat, Reserva Coyhaique, Torres del Paine; buy before you lose signal) and ventaweb.apn.gob.ar (Los Glaciares, card only)</li>\n<li>Torres del Paine park tickets (online only)</li>\n</ul></div>\n<div class="card"><h3>Pack</h3><ul class="clean">\n<li>A properly waterproof jacket <i>and</i> overtrousers, a warm layer, a hat and gloves, even in February</li>\n<li>Trekking poles (for steep moraines), and boots with good grip</li>\n<li>Sunscreen and sunglasses: the UV is strong under the ozone hole</li>\n<li>Insect repellent and long sleeves: <b>tábanos</b> (horseflies) are fierce in Aysén in January</li>\n<li>A water filter, a stove, a power bank and a car charger</li>\n</ul></div>\n</div>\n',
)
